* GOAL Type System::
* GOAL Examples::
@end menu

@node GOAL Lexical Elements
@section Lexical Elements

@subsection Keywords

GOAL recognizes the following reserved keywords (case-insensitive):

@table @code
@item defun
Defines a named function
@item defmethod
Defines a method within a type
@item deftype
Defines a new type structure
@item let, let*
Local variable bindings (let* for sequential binding)
@item if
Conditional branching
@item when, unless
One-branch conditionals
@item cond
Multi-branch conditional (similar to switch)
@item case
Case expression dispatch
@item loop, do, while, until
Iteration constructs
@item quote, quasiquote, unquote
Quotation and meta-programming
@item lambda
Anonymous function definition
@item and, or, not
Logical operations
@item nil, true, false
Boolean and null values
@item set!, setq
Variable mutation
@item return, break, continue
Control flow within loops
@end table

@subsection Identifiers

Identifiers in GOAL follow these rules:

@itemize
@item Start with a letter, underscore, or hyphen
@item Contain letters, digits, hyphens, underscores, and exclamation marks
@item Case-insensitive in source but normalized to lowercase kebab-case
@item Examples: @code{player-health}, @code{enemy_count}, @code{MAX_SCORE!}
@end itemize

The lexer normalizes all identifiers to @strong{kebab-case} for consistent AST representation:

@table @asis
@item @code{PlayerName} @tab @result{} @code{player-name}
@item @code{player_score} @tab @result{} @code{player-score}
@item @code{MAX_HEALTH!} @tab @result{} @code{max-health!}
@item @code{get-value} @tab @result{} @code{get-value}
@end table

@subsection Literals

GOAL supports multiple literal formats for numeric and string values:

@subsubsection Decimal Literals

Plain sequences of digits represent base-10 integers:

@example
42
1000
0
@end example

@subsubsection Hexadecimal Literals

Hexadecimal numbers use the prefix @code{#x}:

@example
#xFF
#x1000
#xDEADBEEF
#x0
@end example

@subsubsection Octal Literals

Octal numbers use the prefix @code{#o}:

@example
#o777
#o1000
#o0
@end example

@subsubsection Binary Literals

Binary numbers use the prefix @code{#b}:

@example
#b1111
#b10101010
#b0
@end example

@subsubsection Dword (Constant String) Literals

Memory or constant strings use the @code{#d} prefix:

@example
#d"WORD"
#d"DATA"
@end example

@subsubsection String Literals

Double-quoted strings represent character sequences:

@example
"Hello, World!"
"Game Over"
""
@end example

@subsection Comments

GOAL uses semicolon-based comments:

@table @asis
@item Line comments
Begin with @code{;} and extend to end of line
@item Block comments
Enclosed in @code{#|} and @code{|#}
@end table

@example
;; This is a line comment
#| This is a
   block comment
   spanning multiple lines |#
@end example

@subsection Operators

@subsubsection Arithmetic Operators

@table @code
@item +
Addition (variadic: @code{(+ 1 2 3)} = 6)
@item -
Subtraction or negation (@code{(- 5 3)} = 2, @code{(- 5)} = -5)
@item *
Multiplication (variadic)
@item /
Integer division
@item %
Modulo (remainder)
@end table

@subsubsection Comparison Operators

@table @code
@item =
Numeric equality
@item ==
Identity/reference equality
@item !=
Numeric inequality
@item <
Less than
@item >
Greater than
@item <=
Less than or equal
@item >=
Greater than or equal
@end table

@subsubsection Bitwise Operators

@table @code
@item &
Bitwise AND
@item |
Bitwise OR
@item ^
Bitwise XOR
@item ~
Bitwise NOT
@item <<
Left shift
@item >>
Right shift (arithmetic)
@end table

@subsubsection Logical Operators

@table @code
@item and
Logical AND (short-circuit)
@item or
Logical OR (short-circuit)
@item not
Logical NOT
@end table

@subsubsection Access Operators

@table @code
@item .
Member/slot access
@item ->
Pointer dereference
@item @
Array/vector indexing (alternative: @code{aref})
@end table

@subsubsection Special Operators

@table @code
@item '
Quote (prevent evaluation)
@item `
Quasiquote (selective evaluation)
@item ,
Unquote (within quasiquote)
@item ,@@
Unquote-splice
@end table

@node GOAL Grammar Overview
@section Grammar Overview

@subsection Top-Level Constructs

A GOAL program consists of top-level forms:

@example
(deftype player
  ((x int)
   (y int)
   (health int)))

(defun move (p dx dy)
  (set! (. p x) (+ (. p x) dx))
  (set! (. p y) (+ (. p y) dy)))

(defun main ()
  (let ((hero (new player)))
    (move hero 10 5)
    (print (. hero health))))
@end example

@subsection Statement and Expression Forms

GOAL is expression-oriented; all forms return values. Major categories:

@itemize
@item @strong{Definitions}: defun, defmethod, deftype
@item @strong{Bindings}: let, let*, lambda
@item @strong{Conditionals}: if, when, unless, cond, case
@item @strong{Loops}: loop, do, while, until, for
@item @strong{Assignments}: set!, setq
@item @strong{Calls}: function application via parentheses
@item @strong{Quoting}: quote, quasiquote, unquote
@item @strong{Control}: return, break, continue
@end itemize

@subsection AST Mapping

GOAL source maps to EIGHTBOL canonical AST as follows:

@table @asis
@item @code{(+ a b)}
@result{} @code{(:compute :op :+ :operands (a b))}
@item @code{(set! x 5)}
@result{} @code{(:move :target x :source 5)}
@item @code{(if cond (then-form) (else-form))}
@result{} @code{(:if :condition cond :then then-form :else else-form)}
@item @code{(loop (form1) (form2))}
@result{} @code{(:perform :target loop-label)}
@end table

@node GOAL Statements
@section Statements

@subsection Function Definition

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(defun name (param1 param2 ...) [docstring]
  form1
  form2
  ...)
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Defines a named function. Parameters are bound for the duration of the function. The final form's value is returned. Optional docstring documents behavior.

@strong{Examples}:

@example
(defun square (x)
  "Return the square of x"
  (* x x))

(defun add-3 (a b c)
  (+ a b c))

(defun greet (name)
  (print "Hello, ")
  (print name)
  (print "!"))
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:invoke :method "name" :using (param1 param2 ...))}

@subsection Type Definition

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(deftype name
  ((field1 type1 [initval])
   (field2 type2 [initval])
   ...))
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Defines a structured data type with named fields. Fields have types and optional initial values.

@strong{Examples}:

@example
(deftype point
  ((x int 0)
   (y int 0)))

(deftype actor
  ((position point)
   (velocity point)
   (health int 100)
   (name string "Actor")))
@end example

@strong{AST}: Maps to record type definition in EIGHTBOL intermediate representation

@subsection Local Binding

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(let ((var1 expr1)
      (var2 expr2)
      ...)
  body)

(let* ((var1 expr1)
       (var2 (+ var1 10))
       ...)
  body)
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: @code{let} binds all variables to their initial expressions in parallel (all RHS evaluated before binding). @code{let*} binds sequentially (earlier bindings visible in later expressions).

@strong{Examples}:

@example
(let ((x 10)
      (y 20))
  (+ x y))
;; Result: 30

(let* ((x 10)
       (y (+ x 5)))
  (list x y))
;; Result: (10 15)

(let ((a (read-input))
      (b (+ a 100)))
  (print "Values: " a ", " b))
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:invoke :method "let" :using ((var1 expr1) ...))}

@subsection Conditional Expression

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(if test-form then-form else-form)
(if test-form then-form)

(when test-form form1 form2 ...)
(unless test-form form1 form2 ...)

(cond (test1 result1)
      (test2 result2)
      (else result3))

(case selector
  (value1 result1)
  (value2 result2)
  (else result3))
@end example

@strong{Semantics}:
@itemize
@item @code{if}: Evaluates condition; if true, evaluates then-form, otherwise else-form
@item @code{when}: Executes body only if test is true
@item @code{unless}: Executes body only if test is false
@item @code{cond}: Evaluates test clauses in order; executes first matching clause
@item @code{case}: Dispatches on selector value
@end itemize

@strong{Examples}:

@example
(if (> health 0)
    (print "Still alive!")
    (print "Game Over"))

(when (< x 10)
  (set! x (+ x 1))
  (print "Moved"))

(cond ((> score 1000) (print "Excellent!"))
      ((> score 500)  (print "Good!"))
      (else           (print "Try again!")))

(case player-class
  (WARRIOR (add-strength 10))
  (MAGE    (add-mana 20))
  (else    (print "Unknown class")))
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:if :condition test :then then-form :else else-form)}

@subsection Iteration

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(loop form1 form2 ...)
(do (steps) (until-condition) body)
(while condition body)
(until condition body)
(for (var start end [step]) body)
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Execute body repeatedly. @code{break} exits loop; @code{continue} starts next iteration. @code{loop} is infinite; @code{while}/@code{until} are conditional.

@strong{Examples}:

@example
(let ((i 0))
  (while (< i 10)
    (print i)
    (set! i (+ i 1))))

(for (x 0 100 2)
  (print x))  ;; Prints: 0, 2, 4, ..., 98

(loop
  (let ((input (read-line)))
    (when (string=? input "quit") (break))
    (process input)))
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:perform :target loop-label)}

@subsection Variable Assignment

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(set! variable value)
(setq variable value)
@end example

@strong{Semantics}: Assigns value to variable (destructive update). @code{set!} is standard Scheme-style assignment; @code{setq} is an alias for compatibility.

@strong{Examples}:

@example
(let ((x 10))
  (set! x 20)
  x)
;; Result: 20

(set! (. player x) 100)  ;; Slot access
(set! (aref array i) value)  ;; Array access
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:move :target variable :source value)}

@subsection Return and Exit

@strong{Syntax}:
@example
(return value)
(break)
(continue)
@end example

@strong{Semantics}:
@itemize
@item @code{return}: Exit current function with value
@item @code{break}: Exit current loop
@item @code{continue}: Jump to next loop iteration
@end itemize

@strong{Examples}:

@example
(defun first-positive (lst)
  (dolist (item lst)
    (when (> item 0)
      (return item))))

(loop
  (when (condition?) (break))
  (process-item)
  (continue))
@end example

@strong{AST}: @code{(:invoke :method "return" :using value)}

@node GOAL Expressions
@section Expressions

@subsection Function Calls

GOAL uses prefix notation for function calls:

@example
(function-name arg1 arg2 ...)
(+ 1 2 3)
(print "Hello")
(max a b c)
@end example

Nested calls:

@example
(+ (* 2 3) (- 5 1))
;; = (+ 6 4) = 10
@end example

@subsection Arithmetic Expressions

@example
(+ 10 20 30)        ;; = 60
(- 100 25)          ;; = 75
(* 2 3 4)           ;; = 24
(/ 100 4)           ;; = 25
(% 17 5)            ;; = 2
(- 10)              ;; = -10 (negation)
@end example

@subsection Comparison Expressions

@example
(= 5 5)             ;; true
(< 3 5)             ;; true
(> 10 5)            ;; true
(<= 10 10)          ;; true
@end example

@subsection Logical Expressions

@example
(and (> x 0) (< x 100))
(or (= state IDLE) (= state WAITING))
(not (null? item))
@end example

@subsection Bitwise Expressions

@example
(& #xFF #x0F)       ;; Bitwise AND
(| #x80 #x01)       ;; Bitwise OR
(^ #xAA #x55)       ;; Bitwise XOR
(<< 1 4)            ;; Left shift (1 << 4 = 16)
(>> #x80 2)         ;; Right shift
@end example

@subsection Lambda (Anonymous Functions)

@example
(lambda (x) (* x x))
(lambda (a b) (+ a b))
((lambda (x) (* x 2)) 5)  ;; Direct invocation = 10
@end example

@subsection Operator Precedence

In GOAL's prefix notation, parentheses explicitly control evaluation order. There is no implicit precedence; the leftmost form is the function, followed by arguments.

@example
(+ 1 2)             ;; 1 is the function +, args are 2
(max 5 10 3)        ;; 5 is the function max, args are 10, 3
@end example

@node GOAL Type System
@section Type System

@subsection Primitive Types

@table @code
@item int
Machine integer (typically 16 or 32 bits)
@item float
Floating-point number
@item string
Character sequence
@item symbol
Interned symbol (immutable name)
@item boolean
True or false value
@item null
Absence of value
@end table

@subsection Compound Types

@table @code
@item list
Linked list of values
@item vector
Fixed-size array
@item record
Structure with named fields
@item function
Callable object
@end table

@subsection User-Defined Types

User-defined types are created with @code{deftype}:

@example
(deftype rect
  ((x int)
   (y int)
   (width int)
   (height int)))
@end example

Access fields with dot notation:

@example
(. my-rect x)
(set! (. my-rect width) 100)
@end example

@subsection Type Compatibility

GOAL performs implicit conversion between types where reasonable:

@itemize
@item Numeric types coerce to broader ranges (int @result{} float)
@item Strings convert to symbols for compatibility
@item Lists may coerce to vectors in array contexts
@end itemize

@node GOAL Examples
@section Examples

@subsection Complete Program Example

@example
(deftype entity
  ((x int 0)
   (y int 0)
   (vx int 0)
   (vy int 0)
   (active boolean true)))

(defun update-entity (e)
  "Update entity position by velocity"
  (set! (. e x) (+ (. e x) (. e vx)))
  (set! (. e y) (+ (. e y) (. e vy))))

(defun collides? (e1 e2 radius)
  "Check if two entities collide within radius"
  (let ((dx (- (. e1 x) (. e2 x)))
        (dy (- (. e1 y) (. e2 y))))
    (< (+ (* dx dx) (* dy dy))
       (* radius radius))))

(defun main ()
  "Game loop"
  (let ((player (new entity))
        (enemy (new entity))
        (running true))
    (set! (. player x) 50)
    (set! (. player vx) 2)
    (set! (. enemy x) 200)
    (set! (. enemy vy) -1)
    (loop
      (update-entity player)
      (update-entity enemy)
      (when (collides? player enemy 10)
        (print "Collision detected!")
        (set! running false))
      (when (not running)
        (break)))))
@end example

@subsection Canonical Use Cases

@strong{Pattern: Processing a List}:
@example
(defun sum-list (lst)
  (let ((total 0))
    (dolist (item lst)
      (set! total (+ total item)))
    total))
@end example

@strong{Pattern: Conditional Update}:
@example
(defun apply-bonus (player bonus-type)
  (cond ((= bonus-type HEALTH) 
         (set! (. player health) (+ (. player health) 25)))
        ((= bonus-type SPEED) 
         (set! (. player speed) (+ (. player speed) 5)))
        (else 
         (print "Unknown bonus type"))))
@end example

@subsection Best Practices

@itemize
@item Use @code{deftype} to define all structured data types early
@item Prefer @code{let} and @code{let*} for local scoping over global mutations
@item Use @code{when} and @code{unless} for single-branch conditionals; @code{cond} for multi-branch
@item Document functions with docstrings describing parameters and return value
@item Use meaningful kebab-case names; avoid abbreviations
@item Keep functions small and focused; compose larger operations from smaller ones
@end itemize
